WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.300
Here are two clips of
the same flat landscape.

00:00:02.520 --> 00:00:04.180
In one, the music is quick and bright.

00:00:04.320 --> 00:00:05.800
In the other, it's slow and low.

00:00:06.060 --> 00:00:07.780
One sky is yellow, the other blue-grey.

00:00:08.100 --> 00:00:09.800
Pick which sky goes with which music.

00:00:10.140 --> 00:00:12.860
And keep an eye on the strip at
the bottom, which has two scales,

00:00:13.040 --> 00:00:15.580
happy to sad and strong to weak,
because we'll need them.

00:00:35.557 --> 00:00:38.557
I'm Tanmay Kulkarni,
in for Humanitarians AI.

00:00:39.037 --> 00:00:40.877
If you put the yellow
sky on the quick tune,

00:00:40.957 --> 00:00:43.637
you picked the way most listeners do,
in two different countries.

00:00:44.137 --> 00:00:45.297
Here's the odd part, though.

00:00:45.517 --> 00:00:47.337
Nothing in a yellow sky sounds quick.

00:00:47.357 --> 00:00:49.077
So what are people actually matching?

00:00:50.130 --> 00:00:50.970
In 2013,

00:00:51.170 --> 00:00:52.910
Stephen Palmer and
colleagues at Berkeley played

00:00:52.930 --> 00:00:55.150
18 pieces of orchestral music by Bach,

00:00:55.390 --> 00:00:57.330
Mozart and Brahms, 50 seconds each.

00:00:57.790 --> 00:01:00.710
Some were slow, some medium,
some fast, in major and minor.

00:01:00.970 --> 00:01:03.970
About 97 people, in the United
States and in Mexico, listened.

00:01:04.290 --> 00:01:07.370
For each piece, they picked the five
colors that fit best from a set of 37,

00:01:07.390 --> 00:01:09.030
and the five that fit worst.

00:01:09.510 --> 00:01:10.810
In both countries, quick,

00:01:10.890 --> 00:01:12.130
major music drew lighter,

00:01:12.590 --> 00:01:14.350
more saturated, yellower colors.

00:01:14.850 --> 00:01:17.790
Slow, minor music drew darker,
greyer, bluer ones.

00:01:18.933 --> 00:01:20.373
Here's what they think is underneath.

00:01:20.593 --> 00:01:22.553
People also rated every
piece, and every color,

00:01:22.573 --> 00:01:25.253
on feelings: happy, sad,
strong, weak and more.

00:01:25.773 --> 00:01:28.113
Rate the music, rate the colors
it drew, and the two line up.

00:01:28.133 --> 00:01:29.553
.97 for happy to sad.

00:01:29.573 --> 00:01:31.353
.96 for strong to weak.

00:01:31.633 --> 00:01:34.273
Across all four word pairs, .89 to .99.

00:01:34.293 --> 00:01:36.573
So the idea is that color
doesn't hear the music.

00:01:36.713 --> 00:01:38.073
Both carry the same feeling.

00:01:38.273 --> 00:01:40.073
One honest limit: these are correlations.

00:01:40.833 --> 00:01:44.313
The authors say so, and they say
a direct link isn't ruled out.

00:01:45.325 --> 00:01:47.365
Run it with the paper's own two examples.

00:01:47.625 --> 00:01:50.225
Bach's fast dance in F major:
rate it happy, strong.

00:01:50.385 --> 00:01:51.685
Which colors sit in that corner?

00:01:51.865 --> 00:01:54.185
The paper says saturated
yellow and light yellow.

00:01:54.665 --> 00:01:57.585
Now Brahms's slow Adagio
in C minor: sad, weak.

00:01:57.805 --> 00:01:59.325
Black, dark grey, dark cyan.

00:01:59.545 --> 00:02:01.045
The step in the middle is the rating.

00:02:01.265 --> 00:02:03.085
The sound never has to touch the color.

00:02:04.101 --> 00:02:07.561
Why would color be ready to carry
feeling at all? In an essay we read,

00:02:07.841 --> 00:02:10.401
babies sort colors into
categories by about 6 months,

00:02:10.421 --> 00:02:12.041
and name them by about 18.

00:02:12.441 --> 00:02:14.221
So the sorting comes before the words.

00:02:14.481 --> 00:02:17.361
A color can mean something to you
before you have a word for it.

00:02:18.248 --> 00:02:19.628
How far does the match travel?

00:02:19.648 --> 00:02:22.268
Between the United States
and Mexico, very far:

00:02:22.408 --> 00:02:24.828
the correlations between the
two countries ran from point

00:02:24.848 --> 00:02:27.328
eight eight to .97 across
the color measures.

00:02:27.668 --> 00:02:29.308
But those are two
countries in the Americas,

00:02:29.328 --> 00:02:31.128
and the music was Western classical.

00:02:31.448 --> 00:02:34.048
The authors write that it's unclear
for music built on other scales.

00:02:34.068 --> 00:02:36.788
And the essay we read adds
that color symbols differ:

00:02:36.948 --> 00:02:40.228
white for purity in much of the West,
mourning in many Eastern cultures.

00:02:40.768 --> 00:02:42.908
So take it as strong, not as a law.

00:02:43.979 --> 00:02:45.219
Now put that to work.

00:02:45.399 --> 00:02:49.079
The essay we read describes how editors
set a rhythm of arousal with scene length:

00:02:49.259 --> 00:02:51.199
long calm scenes, short busy ones.

00:02:51.499 --> 00:02:54.119
Its rule of thumb is a calm
scene of 7 to 12 seconds,

00:02:54.139 --> 00:02:57.299
set against a busy one of 1 to
3. Look at those two strips.

00:02:57.479 --> 00:02:59.319
Same length of film, very different pulse.

00:02:59.559 --> 00:03:02.959
It pairs the calm strip with cool
colors and the busy one with warm.

00:03:03.971 --> 00:03:07.171
Pacing has been tested too,
on its own. In 2011,

00:03:07.311 --> 00:03:11.431
Angeline Lillard and Jennifer Peterson had
60 four-year-olds watch a fast cartoon,

00:03:11.651 --> 00:03:13.971
watch a slower one,
or draw, for 9 minutes.

00:03:14.171 --> 00:03:15.931
Right afterwards, the fast group did worse

00:03:15.951 --> 00:03:18.091
on four tasks of
self-control and planning.

00:03:18.411 --> 00:03:20.331
That test was about pacing, not color,

00:03:20.511 --> 00:03:23.291
it measured the immediate effect,
and it was one group of four-year-olds.

00:03:24.710 --> 00:03:27.110
Here's a case where two things
pull against each other.

00:03:27.590 --> 00:03:30.290
Fast music pulls toward yellow,
and minor pulls toward blue.

00:03:30.490 --> 00:03:33.710
The paper played every combination,
so fast and minor is in the data.

00:03:33.990 --> 00:03:37.650
Use the strip: fast is strong, minor is
sad, so where does fast and minor land?

00:03:37.770 --> 00:03:39.450
Then answer two more questions about it.

00:03:39.470 --> 00:03:41.770
Is its color lighter or
darker than slow minor's?

00:03:41.950 --> 00:03:44.050
Is it more or less
saturated than fast major's?

00:03:44.390 --> 00:03:46.290
Pause here and write down both answers.

00:03:53.262 --> 00:03:54.962
Here's what the paper's chart shows,

00:03:54.982 --> 00:03:57.402
read off its figure for the
United States listeners.

00:03:58.222 --> 00:03:59.862
Fast, minor music drew colors lighter,

00:03:59.962 --> 00:04:03.542
more saturated and yellower than
slow minor, and less than fast major.

00:04:03.982 --> 00:04:07.542
Tempo and mode each moved the colors on
their own, and fast minor landed between.

00:04:07.722 --> 00:04:09.402
If you guessed between, the method held.

00:04:09.622 --> 00:04:12.462
If you guessed one pole, that's
where it needs more than two scales.

00:04:16.510 --> 00:04:18.330
So here's the opening again, swapped.

00:04:18.510 --> 00:04:20.130
The quick tune under the blue-grey sky.

00:04:20.330 --> 00:04:21.890
The slow tune under the yellow one.

00:04:21.950 --> 00:04:23.910
It feels wrong, and now you know why.

00:04:24.130 --> 00:04:25.710
It isn't that the colors can't hear.

00:04:25.930 --> 00:04:29.050
It's that each of them is carrying a
feeling, and the feelings don't agree.

00:04:38.059 --> 00:04:41.139
I'm Tanmay Kulkarni,
in for Humanitarians AI.

00:04:41.619 --> 00:04:44.639
The study is Palmer's, and it
all began with an essay we read.
